  "genes": [
    {
      "type": "formulation",
      "text": "Replace smooth-max ascent with sequential minimax optimization. Set c=f*f and Q=(2 sum c²+sum adjacent products)/3. Linearize log Q−2 log(sum f)−log t, enforce c+2(f*δf)≤t and f+δf≥0, and impose a trust region. Accept steps only when the exact verifier score improves."
    },
    {
      "type": "numeric",
      "text": "Use constraint generation for minimax subproblems: initially include convolution lags within 1e-7 of the maximum, solve the restricted problem, then scan every lag using FFT convolution. Add violated constraints and repeat. Retain previously binding lags across iterations to prevent cycling between competing peaks."
    },
    {
      "type": "structure",
      "text": "Optimize one multiplicative weight per existing nonzero run before optimizing individual cells. Preserve each run's internal profile and all zero gaps. Compute run-weight derivatives by summing cell derivatives weighted by the incumbent values, and periodically release only runs showing the largest predicted gains."
    },
    {
      "type": "formulation",
      "text": "Optimize selected amplitudes directly with nonnegative bounds instead of squaring unconstrained variables. Include currently zero cells adjacent to run boundaries, whose derivatives vanish under the squared parameterization. Restrict newly activated mass with a small aggregate budget, then increase that budget only after exact-score improvements."
    },
    {
      "type": "numeric",
      "text": "Choose LogSumExp sharpness from its observed approximation error rather than a fixed schedule. After normalizing the convolution maximum to one, increase sharpness until log(sum exp(beta*(c−max c)))/beta is below 1e-8. Compare exact scores after every accepted iteration and restart from the best exact point after deterioration."
    },
    {
      "type": "structure",
      "text": "Search coordinated packet translations rather than isolated support moves. Select two to eight nonzero runs, propose opposing shifts that preserve their internal amplitudes and avoid overlap, and jointly reoptimize their weights. Rank proposals by predicted changes at near-maximal convolution lags before spending an exact FFT evaluation."
    },
    {
      "type": "structure",
      "text": "Build a multiresolution amplitude basis within each nonzero run: begin with one scaling coefficient, then add zero-sum left-versus-right contrasts recursively. Refine runs with the largest projected ascent derivatives. Preserve the original grid and zero gaps throughout; do not interpolate or resample the incumbent."
    },
    {
      "type": "numeric",
      "text": "Apply Hessian-vector products to the active-constraint tangent space to seek collective escape directions. Approximate products by gradient differences, constrain first-order changes of binding convolution peaks, and test both signs of leading positive-curvature directions. Use geometrically spaced step sizes and retain only exact-score gains."
    },
    {
      "type": "formulation",
      "text": "Estimate nonnegative multipliers on near-maximal convolution lags by minimizing the projected stationarity residual. Use the resulting Lagrangian gradient to rank inactive cells and entire runs, rather than using one arbitrary maximum lag. Test mass transfers between high- and low-reduced-gradient groups with exact-score line searches."
    },
    {
      "type": "structure",
      "text": "Run basin-hopping with support-preserving, correlated perturbations: multiply groups of neighboring runs by reciprocal random factors, renormalize, and locally polish each proposal. Sweep perturbation strengths from 1e-4 to 1e-1 in log amplitude. Maintain a separate best-exact archive even when temporarily accepting worse basins."
    }
  ]
}
